From 89ffb19309193edb99dfda7def3e205239afe261 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 12:06:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Stop a promise job from running JavaScript beside the
 JavaScript that queued it

`await 0` queues a job, so the statements after a synchronous call to an
async function belong to the job already running. Measured, they did not:

    var out = 'no'; var v = 1;
    var f = async function () { v = v + 1; out = v; await 0; v = v + 10; };
    f();
    String(out) + ',' + v      // "2,2" required; "2,12" observed

The continuation ran, and it ran on another thread. Both dispatch paths
for a job were wrong, for opposite reasons:

  - no ambient SynchronizationContext -- every plain Eval -- went to
    ThreadPool.QueueUserWorkItem;
  - one present -- every xUnit test -- went to
    SynchronizationContext.Current.Post, and a test host's context is not
    a JavaScript thread; xUnit's AsyncTestSyncContext dispatches through
    the pool too.

A job resumes a generator, and resuming runs user JavaScript, so the
engine let two threads execute JavaScript in one context at once.
ECMAScript's agent is single-threaded by construction and this engine is
written throughout on that assumption. The reason it read as a rare flake
rather than as corruption is that the racing job only incremented a
number.

The rule now lives in one place, JSContext.PostJob, and the order of its
cases is the whole of it: the engine's own pump if we are on it; the pump
this work belongs to, captured when the promise or the await was created;
this context's queue while it is executing JavaScript; a host context
with nothing executing; the thread pool with neither.

The captured-pump case is not defensive and dropping it deadlocks
Execute, which is how the first attempt failed. A promise created on the
pump thread can be settled from a pool thread, where
SynchronizationContext.Current is null and the first case cannot see the
pump; the job then took the queue, the task AsyncPump.Run was blocking on
never completed, and the pump spun forever. Only a pump is trusted,
whether current or captured.

The queue cannot strand a job, and that is a property of when it is taken
rather than of a fallback: it accepts only while a JavaScript execution is
in progress -- exactly when there is something to race -- so anything
posted with nothing running keeps its old dispatch, which is what lets a
host Task-backed promise settle long after EvalWithTopLevelAwaitAsync
returned. The depth stays at one for the whole drain so a job that queues
a job takes the queue too, and the return to zero happens under the same
lock as the final dequeue. A nested Eval does not drain.

What is not fixed: a job posted while nothing is executing still takes
the host context or the pool and could land during a later execution.
Closing that means the embedding contract has to name a JavaScript thread
the engine can serialize against, which is an API change rather than a
dispatch site.

Eight new fixtures, written to lose the race deterministically -- a spin
after the call so a racing thread reliably wins. That is the difference
between them and the test that found this, which asserts the same value
and caught the bug 0.6% of the time. Against the unmodified pin they fail
5 of 8; with the fix, 8 of 8. Whole engine suite green (8 093 tests), and
test262-promise-jobs and test262-for-await -- whose ticks-with-* cases
assert exact microtask tick counts -- pass 5/5 and 2/2.
